\newlabel{tab:v9-buckets}{{5}{17}{Length-bucketed \ade {} for v9 (dirty data, $n=500$, seed=42). v9 wins short and medium routes; retrieval-top-1 dominates long routes due to mean-pool collapse.\relax }{table.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Two long-bucket val routes ($>$500\,km) where v9 (red dashed) drifts far from ground truth (green), while the zero-training retrieval-top-1 neighbour (steelblue) tracks GT closely. Mean-pool collapse: v9 has the same five retrieved tokens that produced the steelblue route, but those tokens have been compressed to centroids before the decoder cross-attends to them. This motivates v10's per-step retrieval bank.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:motiv-v9-long}{{8}{18}{Two long-bucket val routes ($>$500\,km) where v9 (red dashed) drifts far from ground truth (green), while the zero-training retrieval-top-1 neighbour (steelblue) tracks GT closely. Mean-pool collapse: v9 has the same five retrieved tokens that produced the steelblue route, but those tokens have been compressed to centroids before the decoder cross-attends to them. This motivates v10's per-step retrieval bank.\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
